          id: '105_5_8_5',
          question: 'Describe the wildlife tourism potential of Tamil Nadu.',
          answer: 'Tamil Nadu has significant wildlife tourism potential, with several protected areas harboring rare and endangered species within the unique ecology of the Western Ghats—recognized globally as one of the world\'s 36 biodiversity hotspots.\n\nMudumalai Tiger Reserve: Part of the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve (India\'s largest biosphere reserve, 5,520 sq km), Mudumalai (in the Nilgiris district) is one of the best wildlife sanctuaries for viewing elephants, tigers, leopards, gaur (Indian bison), and diverse bird life. Its position at the confluence of three states (Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Kerala) and its connection to Nagarhole, Bandipur, and Wayanad forests creates a contiguous wildlife corridor—the largest in India. Elephant safari, jeep safari, and guided nature walks are popular wildlife tourism activities.\n\nAnaimalai Tiger Reserve (formerly Indira Gandhi Wildlife Sanctuary): Located in the Pollachi division of Coimbatore district, Anaimalai covers 958 sq km and protects tigers, leopards, wild dogs, elephants, gaur, and rich bird life. The Topslip area is the main visitor zone, offering forest treks and overnight stays in forest rest houses.\n\nKalakkad-Mundanthurai Tiger Reserve (KMTR): South Tamil Nadu\'s most important protected area (895 sq km) in Tirunelveli district. KMTR has one of the highest biodiversity values in India—over 2,000 plant species, 400 bird species, and significant populations of lion-tailed macaque, slender loris, and freshwater fish found nowhere else.\n\nGulf of Mannar Marine National Park: India\'s first marine biosphere reserve, protecting coral reefs, seagrass beds, and marine biodiversity around 21 islands. Dugong, dolphins, whale sharks, and five species of sea turtles inhabit these waters. Eco-tourism in the form of glass-bottom boat rides and snorkeling is carefully managed.\n\nGuindy National Park (Chennai): Located within the city of Chennai, Guindy is one of the world\'s smallest national parks—covering just 2.7 sq km but protecting blackbuck, spotted deer, and 150 bird species.',
