          id: '105_5_8_6',
          question: 'Describe the Meenakshi Temple, Madurai as a heritage tourism destination.',
          answer: 'The Meenakshi Amman Temple at Madurai—dedicated to the goddess Meenakshi (Parvati) and her consort Sundareswarar (Shiva)—is one of India\'s most magnificent temple complexes and a supreme achievement of Dravidian architecture, attracting approximately 15,000–25,000 visitors daily and over 1 million during major festivals.\n\nHistory: The temple\'s origins are ancient—legends trace it to the period of the Pandya kingdom, though the current structure dates primarily from the 16th–17th centuries CE, built and rebuilt under the Nayak rulers of Madurai, particularly Thirumalai Nayak. Major additions (gopurams, mandapams) have continued through subsequent centuries.\n\nArchitecture: The temple complex covers approximately 6 hectares (14 acres) and contains: 14 gopurams (gateway towers)—the tallest, the southern tower, rises 52 metres and is covered with thousands of painted sculptural figures of deities, demons, and mythological scenes; the Thousand-Pillared Hall (actually 985 pillars), now housing the Temple Museum; the Golden Lotus Tank (Porthamarai Kulam), where devotees purify themselves; the Ashta Shakti Mandapam (eight Shaktis gateway); and multiple shrines, halls, and courtyards.\n\nLiving temple: The Meenakshi Temple is not a museum but a living place of worship—open daily from 5 AM to 10 PM, conducting eight daily rituals (pujas) and hosting major festivals year-round. The Chithirai Festival (April–May) celebrating the divine marriage of Meenakshi and Sundareswarar draws hundreds of thousands of devotees from across South India.\n\nTourism management: The HR&CE Department administers the temple. TTDC operates guided tours. The temple earns significant revenue from devotee offerings, ticket sales for the museum, and commercial activities—revenue reinvested in temple maintenance and charitable services.',
